  "## A Welsh schoolbook of the ninth century",
  "In the Bodleian Library at Oxford is a small volume known as St Dunstan’s Classbook. It is four old manuscripts bound together, and two of the four were written in Wales in the ninth century.[[cite:bod-auct]] The first is the Book of Commoneus, Liber Commonei, named after the scribe who signs it. Its Easter tables run from 817 to 835, so it was made in those years, in the age of Merfyn Frych and of the History of the Britons.",
  "It is the notebook of a Welsh school of the church. It has a passage of Isidore on numbers, tables of the moon and of Easter, a treatise on how to reckon them, and readings in Greek and Latin for the great service of the Easter Vigil. Between the lines its teachers wrote the meaning of hard words in Welsh, the Old Welsh of more than eleven hundred years ago.",
  "## The alphabet of Nemnivus",
  "On one of its pages is a set of letters, each with its name, headed with the name of Nemnivus, a scholar of the Britons. Some have taken him to be the same man as Nennius, who gathered the History of the Britons in these very years. Whoever he was, his alphabet shows the learned men of Wales thinking about how their own language should be written.",
  "## Ovid with Welsh notes",
  "!! classbook-ovid",
  "The fourth part of the book is the first book of Ovid’s Art of Love, the great Roman poet, copied in Wales in the ninth century. Three Welsh scribes added notes between its lines, in Latin and in Welsh. It shows that the scholars of Wales read the poets of Rome as well as the fathers of the Church.",
  "## Its journey",
  "By the tenth century the book had crossed to England. Its first page has a drawing of Christ with a monk kneeling at his feet, named in the verses above him as Dunstan, the abbot of Glastonbury who became archbishop of Canterbury, and the drawing is usually said to be his own work. The book stayed at Glastonbury until the end of the Middle Ages. Thomas Allen of Oxford owned it and gave it to the Bodleian Library in 1601, and every page can now be seen online.",
