  "## The house of Sulien",
  "In the eleventh century the church of Padarn at Llanbadarn Fawr, near Aberystwyth, was one of the great schools of Wales. Its teacher was Sulien, twice bishop of St Davids, and his four sons were all scholars. This psalter was made in their school for the eldest son, Rhygyfarch, the author of the Life of Saint David.[[cite:wiki-ricemarch]]",
  "Its text was written by a scribe called Ithael, and its great letters and pictures were painted by Ieuan, Rhygyfarch’s brother. Rhygyfarch himself wrote Latin verses for it. It is one of only two books that survive from the school of Llanbadarn; the other is Ieuan’s copy of Saint Augustine, now in Cambridge.",
  "## What is in it",
  "Its 159 pages hold the psalms of David, the heart of the prayer of every monk and priest, with a letter of Saint Jerome, a list of the apostles, tables, and the Martyrology of Jerome, the calendar of the saints of the whole Church, day by day.",
  "## Its art",
  "Ieuan’s letters are knotwork of many colours, full of twisting beasts and birds, in the old art of the Britons and the Irish, touched by the art of the Norse world of his day. They are among the finest works of art made in Wales before the coming of the Normans, the last flowering of an art that had begun in the age of the saints.",
  "## Its journey",
  "How the book reached Ireland is not known, but it has long been in the library of Trinity College, Dublin. It was printed in full in 1914 as The Psalter and Martyrology of Ricemarch, the Latin form of Rhygyfarch’s name.",
