  "|| Language | Latin",
  "## The year-book of the Britons",
  "The Annales Cambriae, the Annals of Wales, are the oldest chronicle of Wales: a list of years, with a line beside some of them for the things that mattered most, the deaths of kings and saints, battles, plagues and hard winters. They were kept by the clergy of St Davids, and they survive in three medieval manuscripts.[[cite:wiki-annales]]",
  "## Arthur in the annals",
  "Two of their entries are among the most famous lines in the history of Britain. Against the year 516 is written: ‘The battle of Badon, in which Arthur carried the cross of our Lord Jesus Christ on his shoulders for three days and three nights, and the Britons were the victors.’ Against 537: ‘The strife of Camlann, in which Arthur and Medrawd fell; and there was a great death in Britain and in Ireland.’ They are the oldest record of Arthur in a chronicle, set down by the Britons themselves.",
  "Beside Arthur stand the other great names of the first centuries of Wales: the death of Dewi, the great plague of 547 ‘in which Maelgwn, king of Gwynedd, died’, the death of Rhodri Mawr at the hands of the Saxons, and in 950 the death of Hywel, ‘king of the Britons’.",
  "!! annals",
  "## The three manuscripts",
